WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.982
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.982 --> 00:00:08.350
Raise your hand if you graduated college.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:10.820
Pretty much everyone.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.060
If you raise your hand,
I want you to take a moment.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.909
Take a moment and reflect on the impact
that that degree is hard on your life.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.910
The network get enabled the opportunities
that it's pulled into reach.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.230
Now, I want you to imagine
your 18 year old self.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.740
Imagine I told them,
you couldn't go to college.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.909
That circumstances have changed and
it wasn't an opportunity for you.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:41.954
How would you feel?

00:00:41.954 --> 00:00:45.057
What would you do?

00:00:45.057 --> 00:00:47.442
How would you build a career or

00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:52.220
design your resume if you
couldn't start with education?

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.670
This is my first time at college.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.719
Now, don't worry,
I didn't lie on my Stanford application.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.740
At age 18, I started working
in London as an apprentice.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.540
I learned and earned.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.100
I trained as an accountant whilst
working full time in restructuring.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:14.610
Fast forward eight years and

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.555
here I am, still on stage at
Stanford Graduate School of Business,

00:01:17.555 --> 00:01:22.870
an evidence that you can go extraordinary
places with an untraditional path.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.810
I succeeded because of
my own traditional path.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:29.760
But it was hard.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.380
It was hard because of prejudices and
attitudes that exist in society,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.480
prejudices and
attitudes that exist in this room today.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.740
Business leaders, parents and teachers are
sending the wrong signal to young people,

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.390
the signal that the only route
to success is a college degree.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.370
Let me tell you a story.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.200
Shortly after my promotion to
manager at a top consulting firm,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.180
I was rushing from one
meeting to the next.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.510
I ran into one of my favorite
high school teachers.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:09.450
We chatted and he asked me the inevitable,
the dreaded question.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.050
Where did you go to college?

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.720
I didn't, I replied, I'm an apprentice.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:21.710
But before I could finish my sentence, I
saw looking at disappointment in his eyes.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.590
I've lost track of the number
of times I've been told.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:32.060
You need to go to college to get a good
degree or you had so much potential.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.350
Rather than celebrating the successes of
people that have taken an untraditional

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.739
path, we often make them feel inadequate.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.680
Despite my professional success,
I often feel inadequate.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.650
So you might be sat there wondering,
well, why should I care?

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.770
And I say you should care for two reasons.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:59.660
As future business leaders,
you are going to miss out on great talent.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.310
And as humans, you have a responsibility
to the young people in your lives.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.720
So because I'm an MBA student, I'm
going to start with a business case and

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.020
then talk about the human case.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:17.102
So the business case,
we are in an employee market,

00:03:17.102 --> 00:03:21.862
organizations are struggling
to fill their ranks.

00:03:21.862 --> 00:03:25.589
In 2015,
5 million US jobs went unfilled, but

00:03:25.589 --> 00:03:29.160
8.5 million Americans were looking for
jobs.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:31.880
Anybody else see the mismatch?

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.590
Talented people locked
out of the workplace.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.810
Workplaces struggling to
find talented people.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.080
It is in no one's best interest to
only recruit from traditional paths.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.815
By broadening your criteria,

00:03:46.815 --> 00:03:51.705
you're opening yourself to capable and
diverse candidates.

00:03:51.705 --> 00:03:56.973
80% of job applications in the United
States ask for an associate's degree or

00:03:56.973 --> 00:04:01.090
higher if 42% of high school
sophomores graduate college.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.500
Do you see the mismatch?

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.220
I get it, it's hard to drive change.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:14.010
It is hard to open up systems that
include non-traditional backgrounds.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.980
It's hard, but it's doable.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.590
And you can't afford not to.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.131
By opening up your recruitment criteria,
you

00:04:23.131 --> 00:04:27.980
are giving yourself a competitive talent
advantage in this tight employment market.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.100
Why not be the first mover
in this current talent war?

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.270
Now, let's talk return on investment.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.080
I urge you to become the creators and not
just the consumers of tomorrow's talent.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:48.590
By that, I mean, we all have
a responsibility to not just use skills,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:49.710
but build skills.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.220
There is more than just
a business case for

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.050
employing people with
an untraditional background.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.590
There's a business case to
build apprenticeship programs.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:06.630
And this business case is built upon
the value added work that you will get

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.180
from teams that work on your projects.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.049
It's from the value adds that
is given from diversify teams.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.590
We know that there is return on investment
that comes with apprenticeship models.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.930
It has been proven, and it's been proven
to come in as little as 18 months.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.350
So although that there is costs
associated with apprenticeship programs,

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.540
things like wages, training costs and

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.990
program management fees, you will
see a positive return on investment.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.310
Businesses need to push
change from the top down.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.750
As humans,
we need to push change from the bottom up.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:50.770
College for
all is disadvantaging many young people.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:54.190
It is having a detrimental effect on
those that learn better by doing.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.370
It's disadvantaging those
that don't have the means or

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.210
the opportunity to attend college.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.950
Apprenticeship programs allow
people to learn and earn.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.960
Giving opportunities to people
from all walks of life.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.150
Apprenticeship programs can be
undertaken in healthcare, construction,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:19.770
finance, technology, media, even law,
there is more than one way to success.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.500
There are multiple paths, but

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.830
they're currently underfunded and
under recognized.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.590
We are over indexing on one.

00:06:28.168 --> 00:06:33.480
So my ask to you as future business
leaders is, why miss out on great talent?

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.190
Why not push for change and
put your money where your need is?

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:42.570
Why not develop programs to hire and train
the future leaders of your organization?

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.120
And as humans,
we need to challenge our elitist attitudes

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.280
that equate not having
a degree to not being capable.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.770
We need to recognize that
apprenticeship programs and

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:58.549
work experience are just as
valuable as college degrees.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:07.023
So I'm going to leave you
with three challenges.

00:07:07.023 --> 00:07:09.776
I challenge you to change the way you
think about non-traditional backgrounds.

00:07:09.776 --> 00:07:12.930
I challenge you to change
the way you talk about success.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.975
And finally,
next time a resume crosses your desk,

00:07:15.975 --> 00:07:19.641
I challenge you not to jump
straight to education but instead,

00:07:19.641 --> 00:07:23.746
dig into experience, and
benefit from a diverse, young workforce.

00:07:23.746 --> 00:07:25.790
Thank you.

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:29.688
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:07:29.688 --> 00:07:31.139
[MUSIC]

